WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:09.168
Your store keeps three copies of every account on three nodes, so one dead machine loses nothing.

2
00:00:09.168 --> 00:00:10.702
Each copy is a replica.

3
00:00:10.702 --> 00:00:12.902
The number of copies is called N.

4
00:00:12.902 --> 00:00:13.969
Here N is three.

5
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:19.540
Every value carries the timestamp of its write, so you can always tell which copy is newer.

6
00:00:19.767 --> 00:00:27.270
A write that waits for all three is as slow as the slowest replica, and fails while any replica is down.

7
00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:29.507
So it waits for only W of them.

8
00:00:29.507 --> 00:00:30.517
Here W is two.

9
00:00:30.733 --> 00:00:32.054
Replica C is down.

10
00:00:32.054 --> 00:00:35.794
The ledger writes a new balance, 7,000, at time t2.

11
00:00:35.794 --> 00:00:38.875
The coordinator sends it to every replica.

12
00:00:38.875 --> 00:00:43.496
Replicas A and B store it and reply, and the ledger hears back.

13
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:47.570
Then C comes back, still holding 10,000 from time t1.

14
00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:50.711
Its copy is stale, and nothing has told it.

15
00:00:50.933 --> 00:00:53.428
Now the fraud check reads the balance.

16
00:00:53.428 --> 00:00:57.368
The coordinator asks R replicas and keeps the newest answer.

17
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.428
With R equal to one, it might ask only C, and get the old 10,000.

18
00:01:02.633 --> 00:01:03.309
So ask two.

19
00:01:03.309 --> 00:01:09.879
Two of the three replicas have the new balance, and any two replicas you pick include at least one of them.

20
00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:14.618
Two plus two is four, more than three, so the groups always overlap.

21
00:01:14.618 --> 00:01:18.007
That's the quorum rule: R plus W is greater than N.

22
00:01:18.233 --> 00:01:19.851
Say it asks B and C.

23
00:01:19.851 --> 00:01:26.887
B's value is from t2 and C's is from t1, so the coordinator returns B's, the newer one.

24
00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:32.290
The coordinator now knows C is behind, so it sends C the value from t2.

25
00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:33.679
That's read repair.

26
00:01:33.679 --> 00:01:35.653
Now all three copies agree.

27
00:01:35.867 --> 00:01:40.178
Smaller R or W answers faster and survives more failures.

28
00:01:40.178 --> 00:01:45.245
Keep R plus W above N, and a read can't miss an acknowledged write.
